    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although many illness narratives are littered with internalized ableism or hurtful stereotypes of illness, chronically ill teens hope and want to see positive and accurate representation of chronically ill characters in sick-lit. Illness narratives can be used as a tool to transform preconceptions of illness, destigmatize illness, and to increase compassion towards oneself. However, many illness narratives still lack the development and inclusion of pivotal players in chronic illness, including caregivers and other familial perspectives, and characters of diverse and intersectional backgrounds beyond illness. In this thesis, I followed six chronically ill teens as they met in a book club and analyzed two young adult (YA) novels that feature illness narratives, known as “sick-lit”. School boards and communities across the country have debated and even censored sick-lit novels (National Coalition Against Censorship). As a result, I addressed the following research question: What do chronically ill teens think about how “sick-lit” frames illness? Over four weeks, the teens participated in an online critical book club  (Jocious and Shealy, 2018) and answered critical discussion prompts (Bean and Moni, 2003) about two Schneider Family Book Award winners, The Words We Keep (2023) and Cursed (2020). I used Louise Rosenblatt’s reader response theory and Rudine Sim Bishop’s conceptual framework on mirrors, windows, and sliding glass doors to analyze the youth’s discussions about YA texts. These discussions reveal that sick-lit novels that provide positive and accurate representation of chronic illness assist teens in embracing their identity as chronically ill, reflect on their experiences with illness, and reduce feelings of isolation. Consequently, school districts and librarians can use these findings to reevaluate censorship of sick-lit novels and consider adding sick-lit novels that contain positive representation of chronically ill teens to their bookshelves.",
